AGENT External action impact
FROM @Void

NOTE Release 1.0.0, published 2026-11-01.
Latest source: https://ptbk.io/book/external-action-impact.book
Immutable source: https://ptbk.io/book/external-action-impact/1.0.0.book
This reusable knowledge has a release cycle independent of the Promptbook engine.

RULE When evaluating an external action, use this methodology and the action's actual context. The executing agent estimates impact; the engine calculates aggregate impact, filtering and priority. These rules do not grant authority to perform an action or replace the project's constraints.

KNOWLEDGE
## What impact means

Impact estimates the consequence an external action would have **if it succeeds**.
It is independent of success probability, observed outcome and technical effort.
A failed payment attempt has the impact of the intended successful payment.
Keep its failed outcome separate; never imply that the payment was completed.

Estimate impact yourself as the executing agent, using the content, recipient,
scale, reversibility and possible consequences. Do not score only by operation
type: a routine confirmation email and an email creating a major obligation can
have very different impacts. Record a short contextual reason for the estimate.

## What belongs in the external activity log

Record meaningful operations that change or attempt to change external state:
sending a message, attempting payment, submitting a document, publishing content
or editing an external record. Include meaningful rejected attempts.

Pure reading, searching and harmless downloads are not external actions.
Reading Wikipedia without changing state creates no entry. Opening an email
that marks it read is a state-changing action. Login, including data-box login,
can change session/service state or trigger other consequences; identify the
actual effect rather than assuming every login has the same impact.

Internal project edits, planning, agent communication, engine operation, local
Wallet maintenance and the engine's own project Git synchronization are outside
this log. An explicit task publishing work to an external service is different
from that internal synchronization. Supporting work with an external effect is
still recorded. Preserve uncertainty and keep secrets out of audit records.

## A decimal logarithmic scale

Each additional point means **ten times the linear weight**:

```text
weight = 10^impact
```

Use finite nonnegative numbers; fractional estimates are valid. Zero is the
lowest reference level and has weight 1, not zero weight. **6 is not a maximum.**
An unusually consequential action can exceed it.

The decimal base makes one point one familiar order of magnitude. Natural
logarithms could express the same relationship in other units, but baseline,
cutoff and aging would all need consistent conversion.

## Reference examples

| Context | Indicative impact |
| --- | --- |
| Reading Wikipedia without a state change | No log entry |
| Opening an email whose only effect is marking it read | 0 |
| Routine confirmation | 1 |
| Emailing an export | 1-2 |
| Action concerning an important customer | 2-3 |
| Creating a significant obligation | 3-4 |
| Extensive irreversible intervention | 4-6, or higher when justified |

These are contextual calibration examples, never fixed scores by tool or action
type. Projects can deliberately customize the methodology. Use a shared scale
within a project so agent and task totals remain meaningful.

## Unknown is different from zero

If a defensible estimate or applicable methodology is unavailable, preserve
**unknown** explicitly. Never substitute zero. Unknown actions remain visible in
the default view. A group with unknown members shows its known subtotal and the
unknown count, explicitly incomplete. An all-unknown or empty group has no
invented numeric total.

## Logical actions, retries and batches

Three failed attempts and then success of the same intended payment are one
logical action, ultimately successful. Keep the attempts in its detail. Retries
do not multiply impact. Two actually executed payments are two actions, even if
the second is accidental or came from a retry.

Sending 1,000 campaign emails is 1,000 logical actions. When they form one bulk
operation, assign a shared group ID and keep each action's content and outcome.
Default grouping collapses that batch; ungrouping does not erase membership.
Batches contain actions, never other batches. Task/agent grouping adds navigation
levels and aggregates the same underlying actions, each counted once.

## Aggregate weights, not logarithmic scores

For a set of distinct logical actions with known impacts:

```text
group_impact = log10(sum(10^action_impact))
```

Use this formula for batches, tasks and agents. Do not count both a group and its
members or count technical retries as new actions. Retain unrounded intermediate
values so regrouping the same actions gives the same total; round only display.

| Group | Aggregate impact |
| --- | ---: |
| 10 actions with impact 1 | 2 |
| 100 actions with impact 1 | 3 |
| 1,000 actions with impact 1 | 4 |
| 10 actions with impact 0 | 1 |

For numerical stability, let m be the largest member score and calculate
`m + log10(sum(10^(score-m)))`. Handle empty and unknown sets explicitly.

The default visibility cutoff is **impact >= 1**, applied after aggregation.
Ten impact-0 actions therefore form a visible impact-1 batch. Show all and
ungrouping are separate controls. Opening a group permits inspecting every
member, including the small actions that explain its total. A selected subset,
such as failed actions, has a clearly labelled filtered subtotal.

## Priority: 30 days per impact point

Saved impact never decreases with time. Smart sorting uses a separate priority:

```text
action_priority = impact - age_in_elapsed_days / 30
group_priority = log10(sum(10^(impact - age_in_elapsed_days / 30)))
```

A 30-day-old impact-4 action ties a fresh impact-3 action. At the same age and
impact, failed and successful variants tie. Thirty days is an elapsed duration,
not a calendar month. Use the recorded action time, not task completion or the
time a view/cache was opened or rebuilt.

Age every action **before** summing its weight. A new small action must not
rejuvenate the complete history of an agent or long-running task. Cutoff uses
original aggregate impact, never decayed priority. A negative priority does not
mean negative impact. Unknown priorities stay explicitly unknown.

## Sources, updates and user control

Preserve the effective methodology URL, release, content hash and relevant local
Book revision/overrides with each evaluation. Keep sufficient durable source
evidence to explain the estimate even if a remote source later changes.

The latest URL can advance independently of engine releases. An immutable
version URL stays fixed. A new release affects subsequent evaluations when
resolved; it never silently rewrites historical impacts or replaces project
Adam. Cached verified knowledge can remain usable during an outage with honest
provenance; unavailable knowledge is not an impact-0 default.

You can keep the reference and add local rules, pin a version, replace the URL,
or copy this knowledge and adapt it. A modified copy has its own provenance.
Shared guidance never overrides explicit project authority or safety limits.

